C o m p i l a t i o n


      Resevoir Operation Problem
       
      Taken from:
      HW3-LinearProgramingInGAMS.docx
      USU CEE 6410
      Dr. Rosenberg
       
      A reservoir is designed to provide water for irrigation. The reservoir has a capacity of 9,000 acre-
      feet. Initial storage is 3,000 acre-feet of water. The size of the diversion canal and farmed area is
      very large relative to the amount of irrigation water available, so there is no upper limit on usable
      irrigation water. Any water above the reservoir capacity must be released to the river (spill). The
      ending storage must be equal to or greater than the beginning storage. The benefits per unit of
      water, and the estimated inflows to the reservoir in 3 months are given in Table 1. What is the
      diversion schedule that maximizes benefits?
       
      Month   |   Inflow (acre-ft)   | Irrigation Benefits ($/acre-ft)
       
      June   |         5,000         |    150
      July   |         3,200         |    170
      August |         2,000         |    425
       
       
      Ammon Wallace
      CEE 6410
      ammon.wallace@usu.edu
  28   
  29  * 1. Define the Sets
  30  Sets
  31  t Time of Month /June, July, August/
  32  loc Location in the Network /Div, Res, Spill/;
  33   
  34   
  35  * 2. Define Input Data
  36  Parameters
  37      c(t) Objective Function Coeffiecients ($ per ac-ft)
  38      /June 150, July 170, August 425/
  39   
  40      inflow(t) Inflow input data (ac-ft)
  41      /June 5000, July 3200, August 2000/
  42   
  43      init_storage /3000/
  44      max_storage /9000/;
  45   
  46  Positive Variables
  47  *    X_div(t) Amount of Water to Divert each month (ac-ft)
  48  *    X_res(t) Amount of Water to keep in the Resevoir at the end of each month (ac-ft)
  49  *    X_spill(t) Amount of Water to spill over each month (ac-ft);
  50      X(loc, t);
  51   
  52  Variables
  53      total_benefits the total amount of profit ($)
  54   
  55   
  56   
  57   
  58  * Defining equations
  59  Equations
  60      Profit
  61      Capacity the Resevoir Capacity
  62      Ending_Storage Ending storage must be greater than initial storage
  63      Mass_balance Mass balance equation;
  64   
  65  * Objective Function
  66  Profit.. total_benefits =E= sum(t, c(t)*X("Div", t));
  67   
  68   
  69  * Constrain Equations
  70  *Capacity(t).. X_res(t) =L= Max_level;
  71  *Protection_level(t).. X_res(t) =G= Min_level;
  72  Capacity(t).. X("Res",t) =L= max_storage;
  73  Ending_Storage(t).. X("Res", "August") =G= init_storage;
  74  Mass_balance(t).. inflow(t) - X("Div",t) - X("Spill",t) =E= X("Res",t) - init_storage$(ord(t) eq 1)  - X("Res",t-1)$(ord(t) gt 1);
  75   
  76  * Create the model
  77  Model Reservoir /all/;
  78   
  79   
  80  * Run the model using Linear method
  81  Solve Reservoir using LP maximing total_benefits;


